<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sharifate of Tazeroualt</h1><p class="st-meta">1613 CE – 1670 CE (approx.) · capital Iligh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A sharifian lordship in the Anti-Atlas, ruled from Iligh, that controlled the Sous and its Saharan trade during the Saadi collapse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hassun-al-semlali">Abu Hassan Ali al-Semlali</a>, a descendant of the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidi-ahmed-ou-moussa">Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa</a>, built a power base in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tazeroualt">Tazeroualt</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-atlas">Anti-Atlas</a>. As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> disintegrated after 1603, he took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a> around 1613 and ruled from Iligh.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sharifate combined the prestige of a holy lineage with control of trade routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, and of Atlantic ports such as Massa. Its ruler dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and French merchants, exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, gold and slaves.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Moulay al-Rashid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a> took Iligh in 1670 and destroyed its power. The descendants of the founder remained influential local lords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tazeroualt">Tazeroualt</a> into the 19th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1613 CE – 1670 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.80%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1613 CE – 1670 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Iligh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Hassan Ali al-Semlali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Tashelhit), Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iligh, Emirate of Tazerwalt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=sharifate-of-tazeroualt" aria-label="Sharifate of Tazeroualt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4491 1029 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sharifate of Tazeroualt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M4858 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4811 1285h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Morocco. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=sharifate-of-tazeroualt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
